This is the "Pro" move.
Junior Devs put try-catch inside every single controller method. Their code looks messy.
Senior Devs write the error handling logic ONCE in a global file, and the whole application uses it automatically.
This is called Global Exception Handling using @RestControllerAdvice.
🟢 Part 1: The Example Project (Bank API)
Scenario: A user tries to withdraw money.
Rules:
 * If the Account ID doesn't exist -> Throw AccountNotFoundException.
 * If Balance < Amount -> Throw InsufficientFundsException.
1. The Custom Exceptions (The "Flags")
First, we create simple classes that represent our errors.
// 1. For when the ID is wrong
public class AccountNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
    public AccountNotFoundException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

// 2. For when money is low
public class InsufficientFundsException extends RuntimeException {
    public InsufficientFundsException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

2. The Global Handler (The "Safety Net") 🛡️
This is the magic file. It sits above your whole app and catches any error thrown anywhere.
@RestControllerAdvice // <--- 1. Tells Spring: "I handle all errors globally"
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    // Catch AccountNotFoundException
    @ExceptionHandler(AccountNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<String> handleNotFound(AccountNotFoundException ex) {
        // Return 404 Not Found + The Error Message
        return new ResponseEntity<>(ex.getMessage(), HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
    }

    // Catch InsufficientFundsException
    @ExceptionHandler(InsufficientFundsException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<String> handleNoMoney(InsufficientFundsException ex) {
        // Return 400 Bad Request + The Error Message
        return new ResponseEntity<>(ex.getMessage(), HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
    }
}

3. The Service (Where we throw the bomb 💣)
Notice: No try-catch here! We just throw the error and let the Global Handler catch it.
@Service
public class BankService {

    @Autowired
    private AccountRepo repo;

    public Account withdraw(Long id, double amount) {
        // 1. Try to find the account
        Account account = repo.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new AccountNotFoundException("Account ID " + id + " does not exist!")); 
            // ^^^ If not found, THROW bomb immediately. Code stops here.

        // 2. Check Balance
        if (account.getBalance() < amount) {
            throw new InsufficientFundsException("You only have " + account.getBalance());
            // ^^^ If low balance, THROW bomb.
        }

        // 3. Success Logic
        account.setBalance(account.getBalance() - amount);
        return repo.save(account);
    }
}

